The live browser check exposed a reconnect repaint problem despite correct decoded pixels. New-stream initialization now resets the canvas backing store; completed frames use a fresh immutable pixel buffer and a CPU-friendly context. Server/client pixel interoperability and reconnect regression tests cover the underlying data. The final live browser check showed the complete character sheet after owned reconnect, with the same 9/9 HP and 0/2000 XP. Immediate 1� and 2� zoom remained crisp. Earlier screenshots with an emulated large viewport showed repaint artifacts; the normal viewport final check passed. No physical-panel pixel comparison is claimed.

## Start your own private playtest

Follow **phase3g-playable-guide.md**, which contains the full procedure and controls. The prepared NUC directory is:

```text
/home/jimbo/pathfinder-playable-phase3g.StnP3cas
```

In your NUC SSH terminal:

```sh
cd /home/jimbo/pathfinder-playable-phase3g.StnP3cas
python3 source/tools/run_private_game.py --server build/apps/server/pfme_server --port 18765 --seed 2
```

The foreground launcher prints a fresh strong secret for that run. Copy it into the browser; it is not stored in a file or committed. In a separate Windows PowerShell terminal:

```powershell
ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes -o BatchMode=yes -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes -L 127.0.0.1:18765:127.0.0.1:18765 jimbo@nuc-server
```

Then open **http://127.0.0.1:18765/play** in a regular browser. Both terminals stay open while playing; Ctrl+C stops their foreground processes. Use the same unused port at both ends of the tunnel.

Controls: Down/S = clockwise, Up/W = counterclockwise, Enter = encoder press, Space = A, Escape = B. Hold Enter for the original 750 ms long press. Rotate to face a tile, then Enter to step. Menus and targeting retain the original state-specific semantics. The onscreen controls emulate the same hardware inputs. Input queues, repeat and focus-loss release are bounded.

## Implementation and limits

The native-only runtime compiles the actual production setup/loop, GPIO input handlers, character systems, room generation, tiles/entities, monster AI, initiative/combat, menus, sheets, graphics and assets. It substitutes only native platform facilities. The only edit under src is removing a redundant, incorrectly capitalized messageLog.h include; the correct header was already included. PlatformIO configuration, firmware data and behavior are preserved.

The renderer uses the original C++ drawing code and original sprites/font on GFXcanvas16. The browser performs lossless RGB565 patch decoding and integer nearest-neighbor scaling. The server serializes gameplay and rendering, stores one immutable pending frame, and preserves the existing 4 KiB request, 16 KiB response, 20 commands/sec and eight-connection limits. Static assets are fixed compiled routes with restrictive headers; gameplay remains authenticated and bound to ownership credentials. No arbitrary filesystem route or client-supplied gameplay state is accepted.

The remaining architectural boundary is explicit: GameSession retains participant capabilities/reconnect lifecycle, while the original handheld globals contain the real world, character, combat and UI. Only one owner can claim that runtime. Other participants cannot view or control it. Independent playable participants, per-player visibility/menus and shared multi-room initiative still require separating those globals. They are not claimed complete. Explicit leave retires the private runtime until process restart, preventing a recycled participant ID from inheriting it.

Audio is silent. Saves/settings use original serialization backed by process-local memory; restart persistence and ESP32 save import are not implemented. Some synchronous flash animation frames can coalesce between snapshots. Physical ST7789 panel gamma, SPI timing, tearing and a physical-device image comparison are not verified. Other classes, advanced spells and puzzles compile from production sources but were not exhaustively playtested.

The native surface uses 115,200 bytes. Screen transfer uses two further such pixel arrays and a bounded patch. The browser keeps a 230,400-byte RGBA accumulator plus canvas, bounded chunks and a transient immutable presentation copy. None of these allocations enters ESP32 RAM. The observed NUC test process used about 7.2 MiB RSS and 0.8% CPU at one sample; this is not a load benchmark.

